exports[`moduleToText > label-diagram: text and markdown snapshots > markdown 1`] = `
"## Anatomy of a display ad

Brightline Media is reviewing Northwind Outdoor's spring banner. Drag each label onto the numbered part of the ad — or tap a label, then a zone. Two labels belong nowhere on the creative.

*[Image: Northwind Outdoor spring sale banner: logo top left, the headline “Trails are calling.”, a round −25% spring gear badge, a backpack and tent illustration, an orange “Shop the sale” button and two lines of small print.]*

- Logo
- Headline
- Offer
- Product shot
- Call to action
- Disclaimer
- Tracking pixel
- Frequency cap

Logo: The logo tells people **who is talking** before they read a word. Most banners get under two seconds of attention, so brand recognition is often the only thing that sticks — keep it clear and in the same corner across every size.

Headline: The headline carries the **one idea** of the ad. Three words read at a glance; anything much longer than six tends to be skipped at banner sizes.

Offer: The offer gives a **reason to act now**. A specific number (−25%) beats vague promises like “great savings”, and it must match what the landing page delivers or the click is wasted.

Product shot: The product shot shows **what is being sold** and sets the mood. One clear hero product reads better at small sizes than a busy collage.

Call to action: The call to action tells people **what happens next**. A high-contrast button with a verb (“Shop the sale”) makes the ad feel clickable and sets expectations for the landing page.

Disclaimer: The disclaimer holds the **terms of the offer**: dates, exclusions and who the advertiser is. Small, but it keeps the ad honest and compliant — so it still has to be legible.

Explanation: Not everything in a campaign is visible: a **tracking pixel** is an invisible 1×1 image that reports views, and a **frequency cap** is an ad-server setting that limits how often one person sees the ad. Neither appears on the creative itself."
`;

