exports[`moduleToText > match-pairs: text and markdown snapshots > markdown 1`] = `
"## Match the metric to its meaning

Tap a metric, then tap its definition — or drag the metric onto the definition. Fewer mistakes, higher score.

| Metric | What it tells you |
| --- | --- |
| CPM | What 1,000 ad impressions cost |
| CPC | What a single click costs |
| CTR | Share of impressions that turned into clicks |
| CPA | What one acquisition (lead or sale) costs |
| ROAS | Revenue earned for every unit of ad spend |
| Frequency | Average number of times one person saw the ad |
| Reach | Number of unique people who saw the ad |
| Conversion rate | Share of visitors who completed the goal |

CPM: Cost per mille. A CPM of $8 means every thousand impressions cost $8 — useful for comparing reach-focused placements.

CPC: Cost per click. Spend ÷ clicks. The Northwind Outdoor account averages $1.40 on search and $0.62 on social.

CTR: Click-through rate. Clicks ÷ impressions. A 2% CTR means 2 clicks for every 100 impressions.

CPA: Cost per acquisition. Spend ÷ conversions. It is the efficiency metric most clients care about.

ROAS: Return on ad spend. Revenue ÷ spend. A ROAS of 4.0 means $4 of revenue for every $1 spent.

Frequency: Impressions ÷ reach. When frequency climbs past 4–5 on social, creative fatigue usually follows.

Reach: Unlike impressions, reach counts each person once — the right metric for awareness goals.

Conversion rate: Conversions ÷ clicks (or sessions). A high CTR with a low conversion rate points at the landing page, not the ad.

You now speak fluent media metrics. The explanations below are worth a second read."
`;

