exports[`moduleToText > what-if-simulator: text and markdown snapshots > markdown 1`] = `
"## Acquisition cost explorer

Drag the sliders to see how budget, bidding and conversion rate shape the cost of every new customer for a fictional paid-search campaign.

Bring **CPA under $30** while keeping spend at or above **$10,000**.

cpa < 30 and spend >= 10000

### Inputs

- Monthly ad spend: Total media budget for the month. (1000–50000)
- Cost per click (CPC): Average price paid for one click. Rises with competition and falls with better ad relevance. (0.2–8)
- Conversion rate: Share of visitors who become customers. Landing-page quality moves this most. (0.005–0.1)
- Average order value: Revenue from a typical first order. (20–500)

### Outputs

- Clicks: Spend ÷ CPC
- Conversions: Clicks × conversion rate
- Cost per acquisition (CPA): What one new customer costs: spend ÷ conversions.
- Revenue: Conversions × average order value
- ROAS: Revenue earned per dollar spent

- Each customer costs more than they spend on their first order. Lower the CPC, lift the conversion rate, or raise the order value before scaling budget.
- ROAS of **3× or more** — every dollar of spend returns at least three in revenue. This is the zone where adding budget usually pays off.
- A CPC above $4 points to crowded auctions or a low Quality Score. Tighter keywords and more relevant ad copy tend to bring it down.

Success: You found a profitable mix. Notice that conversion rate and CPC move CPA far more than raw budget does — budget only scales whatever efficiency you already have."
`;

